(a) The sheriff, or other employee designated by the board of county commissioners (herein referred to as the "designated employee"), shall be authorized to suspend or revoke a license previously granted herein and is further authorized to suspend any employee permit granted hereunder. In the event the designated employee seeks to suspend or to revoke a license or employee permit, written notification shall be given to the applicant or permitted employee of such action. Such notice shall contain a specification of the violations and shall be served upon the applicant. The applicant shall be given written notice of the time and place of the hearing.
(b) The designated employee shall be authorized to suspend or revoke a license or an employee permit in the event of any one or more of the following:
(1) An applicant or permitted employee gave false or misleading information in the original application process or employee permit process;
(2) An applicant or permitted employee has knowingly allowed possession, use, or sale of controlled substances on any premises on which a sexually oriented business operates or knowingly allowed possession, use, or sale of controlled substances by a minor;
(3) An applicant or permitted employee has knowingly allowed the violation of an ordinance of the county or a violation of any criminal law of the state to occur on any premises on which a sexually oriented business operates;
(4) An applicant or permitted employee has violated any provision of this article;
(5) An applicant or permitted employee has been convicted of any drug-related, alcohol-related, or sex-related crime by the state or the county regarding an offense which was committed on the premises, or which would otherwise violate the provisions of this article; and
(6) An applicant fails to pay any fee, occupation tax fine, or other amount of money due the county under this article or any other taxing ordinance of the county.
(c) In the event the designated employee shall suspend or revoke any license or employee permit hereunder, the suspension or revocation shall be for a period of not less than one day nor more than 365 days, within the discretion of the designated employee; provided, however, that the applicant or permitted employee shall be authorized to continue its business operations or employment until that date of the hearing scheduled in accordance with subsection (d) of this section. No applicant may apply for a license during any period of suspension or revocation. In any hearing conducted by the designated employee, the designated employee shall consider, among other things, the severity of the allegations, the evidence submitted, and the testimony presented in making any decision on suspension, revocation, and the duration of either.
(d) In the event of a suspension or revocation by the designated employee, the applicant may appeal the decision to the board of county commissioners by filing a written notice of appeal within ten days from the date of the decision of the designated employee. Thereafter, a hearing shall be scheduled before the board of county commissioners within 45 days after the date of the notice of appeal by the applicant. After the hearing by the board of county commissioners, the board of county commissioners may take such action as it deems appropriate, including the upholding of the action of the designated employee or the imposition of such action as the board of county commissioners may deem appropriate under the facts. The decision of the board of county commissioners shall be final. Appeals from the decision of the board of county commissioners shall be to the county superior court within 30 days of the final action of the board of county commissioners. In the event the applicant does not file an appeal from any decision of the designated employee, as provided herein, the decision of the designated employee shall be final.
(Ord. No. 15-0309A, exh. A(1-15), 3-9-2015)

